(Code 2004, § 155.36; Ord. No. 1710, 7-24-2017)

1074:
The preliminary plat for a subdivision shall be clearly and legibly drawn at a scale of not less than 100 feet to one inch. The sheet size shall not exceed 36 inches by 48 inches; provided, however, a scale of 200 feet to one-inch may be used to avoid sheets in excess of 36 inches by 48 inches. The minimum sheet size shall be 17 inches by 22 inches. At a minimum, the following information and materials should be provided as a part of the preliminary plat application package:
(1) One copy of an accurate and up-to-date boundary survey of the property proposed for subdivision;
(2) One copy of a completed preliminary plat application form;
(3) Six copies of the preliminary plat drawn clearly and accurately by a design professional at a scale appropriate for the nature of the project;
(4) Proposed name of the subdivision;
(5) Names, addresses, and telephone numbers of the property owner of record and the developer or subdivider;
(6) Name, address, and telephone number of each professional firm associated with the preparation of the preliminary plat;
(7) Date of survey, north point, and graphic scale;
(8) Subdivision location including land lot and land district, area in acres, internal and abutting zoning, proposed number of lots with minimum lot size, and proposed phasing, if any;
(9) A location sketch or vicinity map positioning the subdivision in relation to the surrounding area with regard to recognized permanent landmarks. The location sketch scale shall be at a scale of 600 feet to the inch;
(10) Boundary lines of the overall property perimeter showing bearings in degrees, minutes and seconds and distances in feet and hundredths of a foot along all lines and the bearing and distances to an existing street intersection or other recognized permanent landmark. The source of boundary information shall be shown. The boundary survey shall be tied to the state plane coordinate system at two locations along the boundary. Horizontal control point coordinates shall be indicated city subdivision regulations on the preliminary plat;
(11) Current field-run topography with mean sea level contours at intervals no greater than two feet. The source of topographic information shall be shown. The topographic survey shall have a coordinate system based on the Georgia State Plane Coordinate System, East Zone, North American Datum of 1983 (NAD 83). Elevations shown shall be based on the North American Vertical Datum of 1988 (NAVD 88). All measurements and coordinates shown shall use the U.S. Survey Foot definition. A benchmark shall be indicated on the preliminary plat;
(12) Accurate locations of all natural features such as lakes, ponds, streams, creeks, state waters, wetlands, floodplain boundaries and other significant features. The source of floodplain information shall be shown. In addition, the petitioner must provide a copy of the wetland delineation that has been approved by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers;
(13) Accurate locations of all cultural features such as all existing historic resources, cemeteries, rights-of-way, easements, pavements (including widths), bridges, culverts and storm drains, utility lines, appurtenances and structures, city and county jurisdictional limits, land lot and district lines, zoning districts and limits and other significant features;
(14) Proposed layout including lot lines with preliminary dimensions, lot numbers, block letters, street rights-of-way with names and widths, easements, public use facilities, facilities exclusively for subdivision use and all relevant conditions of zoning;
(15) Location of all known existing or previous landfills, or statement that none exist;
(16) Proposed method of potable water supply and sewage disposal; and
(17) Preliminary plat certifications as specified in section 114-104.
(Code 2004, § 155.46; Ord. No. 1710, 7-24-2017)

1081:
The construction plans for a subdivision shall be clearly and legibly drawn at a scale of not less than 100 feet to one inch. The sheet size shall not exceed 36 inches by 48 inches, and the minimum sheet size shall be 17 inches by 22 inches. At a minimum, the following information and materials should be provided as a part of the construction plans application package:
(1) One copy of an accurate and up-to-date boundary survey of the property proposed for subdivision;
(2) One copy of a completed construction plan application form;
(3) Six copies of the construction plans drawn clearly and accurately by a design professional at a scale appropriate for the nature of the project, to include:
(4) Name of subdivision;
(5) Name, address and telephone number of the owner/developer of the property;
(6) Name, address and telephone number of the professional engineer who prepared the plans;
(7) Name, address and telephone number of the designated 24-hour contact person for the project;
(8) Date plans were prepared, north arrow and graphic scale on all appropriate sheets;
(9) Location map at a scale of 600 feet to the inch;
(10) An accurate list of conditions relative to the zoning of the property and the approval of the concept plat and preliminary plat;
(11) Pertinent site development data;
(12) Boundary lines of the overall property perimeter showing bearings in degrees, minutes and seconds and distances in feet and hundredths of a foot along all lines and the bearing and distances to an existing street intersection or other recognized permanent landmark. The source of boundary information shall be shown. The boundary survey shall be tied to the state plain coordinate system at two locations along the boundary. Horizontal control point coordinates shall be indicated on the construction plans;
(13) Current field-run topography with mean sea level contours at intervals no greater than two feet. The source of topographic information shall be shown. The topographic survey shall be tied to USGS elevation benchmark referenced to mean sea level and an elevation benchmark shall be indicated on the construction plans;
(14) Accurate locations of all natural features such as lakes, ponds, streams, creeks, state waters, wetlands, floodplain boundaries and other significant features. The source of floodplain information shall be shown. In addition, the petitioner must provide a copy of the wetland delineation that has been approved by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers;
(15) Detailed limits of clearing and plan for tree protection;
(16) Detailed erosion and sediment control plans;
(17) Detailed grading and drainage and stormwater management plans;
(18) Detailed electric and natural gas utility plans;
(19) Detailed roadway and sidewalk plans that shall include the location of speed cushions with a spacing of approximately 500 feet, clear visibility of 200 feet, and placement no closer than 200 feet from an intersection;
(20) Detailed storm sewer plans;
(21) Detailed water utility plans;
(22) Detailed sanitary sewer plans;
(23) Detailed plans for all amenities, parks, and/or open spaces;
(24) Detailed plans for all telecommunications; and
(25) Such other information as may be required by the city to fully evaluate and review the project.
(Code 2004, § 155.56; Ord. No. 1710, 7-24-2017; Ord. No. 2219, 10-24-2022)

